.TH SYSLOGD 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
syslogd \- system message logger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syslogd
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syslogd
collects the messages that programs send with
.BR syslog (3)
and appends them to log files according to the rules in
.IR /etc/syslog.conf .
.PP
It listens on the
.B AF_UNIX
socket
.IR /var/run/log ,
removing any file already there and making the socket mode 0666.
Substrate's
.B AF_UNIX
sockets are stream-only, so clients connect and send one message per
newline-terminated line; the libc
.BR syslog (3)
client does this.
Up to 32 clients can be connected at once; further connections are
accepted and closed.
A line longer than 2048 bytes is delivered in 2048-byte pieces.
.PP
A message may begin with a priority,
.BI < n >\c
, where
.I n
is the facility code times 8 plus the severity, as in RFC 3164.
Without one, the message is logged as
.BR user.notice .
The priority is removed, and each message is written as
.IP
.I "Mmm DD HH:MM:SS hostname message"
.PP
with the time in UTC and the host name as it was when
.B syslogd
started.
The rest of the message, including any timestamp the sender put in it, is
kept as received.
.PP
For every rule that matches the message, the line (with a newline added if
missing) is appended to the rule's target, which is opened, created with
mode 0640 if necessary, written and closed each time.
.PP
Unless
.B \-f
or
.B \-n
is given,
.B syslogd
detaches from its terminal after creating the socket: it forks twice,
calls
.BR setsid (2),
changes to
.IR / ,
sets the umask to 022 and connects standard input, output and error to
.IR /dev/null .
In either mode it then writes its pid to
.I /var/run/syslogd.pid
and logs
.B "syslogd: started, pid"
.I N
directly to
.IR /var/log/messages .
It is started at boot by
.IR /etc/rc.d/05\-syslogd .
.SS Configuration
Each line of
.I /etc/syslog.conf
that is not blank and does not begin with
.B #
(after leading blanks) is a rule: a selector list, one or more spaces or
tabs, and a target.
The target is the path of a file (or a device such as
.IR /dev/console )
to append to.
At most 64 rules are read.
.PP
The selector list is up to 8 selectors separated by
.BR ; ,
each of the form
.IB facility . level\c
\&.
.I facility
is one of
.BR kern ,
.BR user ,
.BR mail ,
.BR daemon ,
.BR auth ,
.BR syslog ,
.BR lpr ,
.BR news ,
.BR uucp ,
.BR cron ,
.BR authpriv ,
.BR ftp ,
.BR local0 " to " local7 ,
or
.B *
for any facility.
.I level
is one of
.BR emerg " (or " panic ),
.BR alert ,
.BR crit ,
.BR err " (or " error ),
.BR warning " (or " warn ),
.BR notice ,
.BR info ,
.BR debug ,
or
.B *
for any level; a level matches messages of that severity or more severe.
A selector with an unknown name is skipped, and a rule with no valid
selectors is ignored.
A message matches a rule if it matches any of its selectors.
.PP
If
.I /etc/syslog.conf
cannot be opened, or contains no valid rules, these defaults are used:
.PP
.EX
auth.*      /var/log/auth.log
mail.*      /var/log/mail.log
cron.*      /var/log/cron.log
daemon.*    /var/log/daemon.log
kern.*      /var/log/kern.log
user.*      /var/log/user.log
*.*         /var/log/messages
*.emerg     /dev/console
.EE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-n
Stay in the foreground.
.PP
Other arguments are ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Terminated by
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT .
.TP
.B 1
The socket could not be created or bound, or a fork failed.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reread
.I /etc/syslog.conf
and log
.B "syslogd: configuration reloaded"
to
.IR /var/log/messages .
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Close all connections, remove the pid file and the socket, and exit.
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/syslog.conf
Rules.
.TP
.I /var/run/log
Listening socket.
The libc client also tries
.IR /dev/log .
.TP
.I /var/run/syslogd.pid
Process ID of the running daemon.
.TP
.I /var/log/messages
Startup and reload notices are always written here.
.SH EXAMPLE
Log everything of severity
.B err
or worse to one file, and all mail messages to another:
.EX
*.err       /var/log/errors
mail.*      /var/log/mail.log
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR syslog (3),
.BR init (8)
.SH STANDARDS
Accepts the priority prefix of RFC 3164.
There is no network (UDP 514) input or forwarding.
.SH BUGS
A selector with level
.BR none ,
such as
.BR auth.none ,
does not exclude its facility: it makes the whole rule match nothing.
The
.B *.*;auth.none;authpriv.none
rule in the shipped
.I /etc/syslog.conf
therefore never writes to
.IR /var/log/messages .
.PP
Comma-separated facility lists are not supported:
.B auth,authpriv.*
is an unknown facility, and the rule is ignored.
.PP
Messages from the libc
.BR syslog (3)
client carry their own timestamp, so logged lines show two.
.PP
Only file and device targets exist; there are no pipes, remote hosts or
user-terminal targets.
